  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 1245 PM MST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nIt isn't often the case that the lowest confidence forecast period\nis the near term (next 12-24 hours). However, this is the case\ntoday as a weak trough drifts across the state through tonight.\n\nThere is considerable uncertainty in how this afternoon/evening's\nmountain snow showers play out. While CAMs were very bullish with\nprecipitation potential as recently as the 12z cycle, there has\nbeen a notable downtrend since then. Regional/global \ndeterministic guidance hasn't not changed significantly, and \nsupports at least light snow across the mountains beginning in a \nfew hours. One may be wondering, \"why the Winter Weather \nAdvisory?\" Today's forecast and advisory are almost entirely \ndriven by favorable ingredients for heavy snow showers that could \nhave travel impacts despite relatively light snowfall amounts in \nthe grids. Very steep lapse rates (close to 8-9C/km through the \nlowest 6km) and modest mid- level convergence/lift, combined with \nefficient snow ratios could lead to brief periods of 1-2\"/hr \nsnowfall rates. It's possible that these snow showers stick \nmainly to the Mosquito Range, but any snow showers that reach the \nI-70 corridor could lead to brief but difficult travel conditions \nthrough this evening. Essentially this an impacts-driven advisory \nwith a low floor but high ceiling in terms of travel impacts \nthrough the early overnight hours.\n\nWe still look on track to enter a period of mild and dry weather\nfor the rest of the week. A weak ridge is expected to develop to\nour west on Tuesday, leading to a drier northwesterly flow aloft.\nThe ridge should slowly shift eastward and flatten by mid-week\nwhile a strong cutoff low develops over the Ohio River valley.\nTemperatures on Tuesday and Wednesday should return to the upper\n40s to low 50s across the plains. Mid to upper 50s are likely for\nThanksgiving, with additional warming possible to end the week.\n\nForecast confidence decreases on Friday and Saturday as a broad\nswath of moisture embedded in west-northwesterly flow moves \ntowards the forecast area. This moisture is embedded in an area of\nstronger flow aloft, located between a broad trough in south \ncentral Canada and a strong ridge to the west of Baja California. \nThere has been poor consistency (both model to model and \nd[model]/dt) in this period, with the ECMWF generally further \nsouth with the jet and accompanying moisture/lift, while the GFS \nis on the drier side. This is also reflected across their \nrespective ensemble distributions. Interestingly, the AIGEFS has \nshifted notably away from the physics-based GFS/GEFS today, though\nit's not clear if this is an actual trend or run-to-run noise. I \nhave a slight preference towards the wetter (snowier) solutions \nthat would likely bring at least some minor travel impacts to the \nmountains for the start of the weekend.\n\nForecast confidence remains very low by the end of the forecast\nperiod. Guidance generally keeps a very active subtropical jet\nnear the region, with another chance of mountain snow early next\nweek.",
